1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,713
<font color="#000000">This work sharply analyzes the test error of
finite-rank kernel ridge regression,</font>

2
00:00:05,793 --> 00:00:09,242
<font color="#000000">the model behind tuning a frozen network's last layer.</font>

3
00:00:09,322 --> 00:00:14,600
<font color="#000000">It derives matching upper and lower bounds that
stay tight for any regularization.</font>

4
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:18,369
<font color="#000000">Kernel ridge regression helps explain generalization,</font>

5
00:00:18,449 --> 00:00:23,345
<font color="#000000">and tuning a network's last layer behaves like it
with a finite-rank kernel.</font>

6
00:00:23,425 --> 00:00:28,320
<font color="#000000">But classical bounds are far too loose here: they
keep the ridge above zero,</font>

7
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:32,640
<font color="#000000">give only upper bounds, and go vacuous as
regularization vanishes.</font>

8
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:38,144
<font color="#000000">Freezing a pre-trained backbone and retraining only
the final layer is everywhere,</font>

9
00:00:38,224 --> 00:00:40,428
<font color="#000000">and it defines a finite-rank kernel.</font>

10
00:00:40,508 --> 00:00:44,680
<font color="#000000">Yet the theory lags: many results need the input
dimension to grow,</font>

11
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:48,995
<font color="#000000">others fix how the ridge decays, and almost none
give a lower bound,</font>

12
00:00:49,075 --> 00:00:51,660
<font color="#000000">without which tightness cannot be claimed.</font>

13
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:54,214
<font color="#FFFFFF">The contribution is threefold.</font>

14
00:00:54,294 --> 00:00:58,121
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, the bounds improve as the ridge goes to zero.</font>

15
00:00:58,201 --> 00:01:03,229
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, a sharp lower bound matches the upper bound
as samples grow,</font>

16
00:01:03,309 --> 00:01:04,582
<font color="#FFFFFF">so both are tight.</font>

17
00:01:04,662 --> 00:01:08,564
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, experiments show a large gain over prior work.</font>

18
00:01:08,644 --> 00:01:12,095
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key trick: work in the eigenfunction basis,</font>

19
00:01:12,175 --> 00:01:15,100
<font color="#FFFFFF">separating spectrum from sampling noise.</font>

20
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:22,876
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method starts from the kernel-ridge estimator
and splits the test error into a bias term and a variance term,</font>

21
00:01:22,956 --> 00:01:25,191
<font color="#FFFFFF">each bounded with high probability.</font>

22
00:01:25,271 --> 00:01:28,896
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key ingredients: careful algebra on the ridge terms,</font>

23
00:01:28,976 --> 00:01:34,387
<font color="#FFFFFF">a sub-Gaussian covariance concentration inequality,
and a Neumann-series expansion.</font>

24
00:01:34,467 --> 00:01:37,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">That lets the bounds hold for any ridge value.</font>

25
00:01:37,810 --> 00:01:42,588
<font color="#000000">Being a theory paper, the experiments use
controlled synthetic settings.</font>

26
00:01:42,668 --> 00:01:50,009
<font color="#000000">The authors test two finite-rank kernels: a
truncated neural tangent kernel and a constructed low-rank kernel.</font>

27
00:01:50,089 --> 00:01:55,339
<font color="#000000">For each they sweep sample sizes from ten to two
hundred and many ridge values,</font>

28
00:01:55,419 --> 00:01:58,780
<font color="#000000">averaging ten trials with median and quartile bars.</font>

29
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:04,634
<font color="#000000">The new upper and lower bounds bracket the error
across sample sizes on both kernels,</font>

30
00:02:04,714 --> 00:02:07,051
<font color="#000000">and squeeze together as samples grow.</font>

31
00:02:07,131 --> 00:02:13,846
<font color="#000000">Against Bach's bound the gain is stark: the new
bound hugs the true error while Bach's floats far above.</font>

32
00:02:13,926 --> 00:02:15,349
<font color="#000000">In the ridgeless limit,</font>

33
00:02:15,429 --> 00:02:19,530
<font color="#000000">bias reduces to the finite-rank error and variance
to the noise.</font>

34
00:02:19,910 --> 00:02:21,736
<font color="#000000">The paper ablates its bound.</font>

35
00:02:21,816 --> 00:02:24,866
<font color="#000000">The finite-rank error is an irreducible floor,</font>

36
00:02:24,946 --> 00:02:30,242
<font color="#000000">while residue terms shrink at a log-N-over-N rate
and vanish for large samples.</font>

37
00:02:30,322 --> 00:02:35,823
<font color="#000000">Dropped, the simplified bounds fail only in the
small-sample regime, as predicted.</font>

38
00:02:35,903 --> 00:02:42,220
<font color="#000000">A ridge sweep shows the new bound improves toward
the ridgeless limit while the prior worsens.</font>

39
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:46,886
<font color="#000000">The bounds hold with probability at least one minus
two over N.</font>

40
00:02:46,966 --> 00:02:50,074
<font color="#000000">The upper bound decays at a log-N-over-N rate,</font>

41
00:02:50,154 --> 00:02:53,123
<font color="#000000">faster than the square-root Rademacher rate.</font>

42
00:02:53,203 --> 00:02:56,795
<font color="#000000">Variance scales as noise times twice the rank over N.</font>

43
00:02:56,875 --> 00:03:02,270
<font color="#000000">And these are the first finite-rank bounds with a
high-probability lower bound.</font>

44
00:03:02,650 --> 00:03:05,773
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway: finite-rank kernel ridge regression,
behind last-layer fine-tuning,</font>

45
00:03:05,853 --> 00:03:08,977
<font color="#FFFFFF">has sharp bounds, matching upper and lower,</font>

46
00:03:09,057 --> 00:03:12,180
<font color="#FFFFFF">tight for any regularization including none.</font>

47
00:03:12,260 --> 00:03:15,383
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key idea,</font>

48
00:03:15,463 --> 00:03:18,587
<font color="#FFFFFF">working in the eigenfunction basis to separate
spectrum from sampling noise,</font>

49
00:03:18,667 --> 00:03:21,790
<font color="#FFFFFF">is a template others can borrow.</font>
